// Q2 – Task Scheduler: Micro vs Macro

console.log("Start");

// Macrotask: goes to the macrotask (timer) queue
setTimeout(() => console.log("setTimeout callback (macrotask)"), 0);

// Microtask: goes to the microtask queue
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("Promise.then callback (microtask)"));

// Synchronous: runs immediately on the call stack
console.log("Synchronous log");

console.log("End");

/*
Expected output:
  Start
  Synchronous log
  End
  Promise.then callback (microtask)
  setTimeout callback (macrotask)

WHY microtasks run before macrotasks:
- The event loop first runs all synchronous code on the call stack.
- When the stack is empty, it drains the ENTIRE microtask queue (promise callbacks,
  queueMicrotask) before taking the next macrotask (setTimeout, I/O, UI events).
- Even with a 0ms delay, setTimeout's callback is a macrotask, so it waits until
  the current script and all pending microtasks finish.
*/